  :adr/body
  "# ADR-2607197000: Opaque PostgreSQL pool leases

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-19

## Decision

Pool and lease identities are opaque i32 authorities owned by a bounded JVM
table. The consumer never receives the native i64 transport handle and imports
only pool open/acquire/query/release/close operations. Lease tokens are
monotonic and removed before reset, making release one-shot and preventing a
stale consumer from acting on a later borrower's connection.

The native table is intentionally narrow: it owns mutable pool membership and
lease freshness, which current Kotoba components cannot persist between calls.
It delegates SCRAM/TLS authentication, query framing/parsing and pool-return
ROLLBACK/DISCARD to compiled `.kotoba` providers. Release returns a channel to
idle only after reset succeeds; failure closes and evicts it. A pool cannot be
closed while leased, and pool/lease quotas are explicit constructor limits.

## Evidence

Released PostgreSQL 14.19 qualification opens one physical connection, dirties
setting/temp/transaction state through lease one, releases it, denies double
release and stale query, acquires a distinct lease token, denies pool close
while leased, proves clean state on the same physical channel, releases,
closes, denies acquisition from the closed pool and finds no handle leak.

This closes the opaque single-node pool lease slice. Multi-connection sizing,
wait queues and idle/lifetime eviction, credential/certificate rotation,
compatibility matrices and soak remain open."}]
